The biggest loop time killer on real hardware is reading sensors one at a time.

By default, every getCurrentPosition() or IMU read sends its own I2C transaction. Four encoders plus an IMU = 5 transactions = 5 × 5ms = 25ms.

Bulk reading fixes this. Instead of sending one command per read, you send ONE command that fetches ALL cached data at once. Then each getCurrentPosition() just returns the already-fetched value — no I2C wait.

The simplest way to enable it:

List<LynxModule> hubs = hardwareMap.getAll(LynxModule.class);
for (LynxModule hub : hubs) {
    hub.setBulkCachingMode(LynxModule.BulkCachingMode.AUTO);
}

In AUTO mode, the SDK automatically refreshes the bulk cache once per loop iteration. Every hardware read after that just returns cached data — no manual clearBulkCache() calls needed. It's a one-time setup in init() that silently saves 20+ milliseconds per loop.
